{#
	**One card per topic**, which is what the knowledge index is instead of a
	table of rows (XIV-168). The second template this repository ships from a
	module package, after `packages/invoice/templates/dashboard/unpaid.html.twig`,
	and the first one that is a *page body* rather than a card on somebody else's
	page.

	Reached as `@XiviKnowledge/index/cards.html.twig`: a bundle's `templates/`
	directory is a Twig namespace named after the bundle class, so shipping a
	template from down here needed no configuration at all.

	**It lives here because the layout is this module's and the page is not**
	(XIV-178). `templates/module/index.html.twig` keeps everything that is every
	module's, which is the heading, the seven buttons, the filter form, both empty
	states, the count underneath and the pager, and hands the region between the
	empty state and the pager to whatever body the module offered. Ninety-six lines of
	card markup sat in that shared file for a day, behind a branch, which is the
	arrangement that makes the *next* module's index another branch in the same
	file rather than another file.

	**Nothing here names an application route.** `urls[record.id]` and
	`card.listing` arrived built, through `Xivi\Core\Record\RecordPageUrl` and
	`Xivi\Core\Record\RecordListUrl`, because §3 says a module package sees
	`Xivi\Core\` and nothing else and a `path()` call is the one place deptrac
	cannot see that boundary break. `tests/Unit/ModuleTemplatesKeepTheBoundaryTest.php`
	is what says so mechanically.

	**Two of the words below are the application's**, `module.card_shown` and
	`module.card_all`, named with their domain out loud rather than by omission.
	That is deliberate rather than a slip: the linked-records card on
	`templates/module/show.html.twig` says exactly the same two sentences about
	exactly the same situation, and a second copy of "Showing 10 of 47" in four
	languages is a second thing to keep in step for no reason anybody could
	state. Everything this module has an opinion of its own about is in
	`knowledge.<locale>.yaml` beside this directory.

	`record_title()` is core's own Twig function (`Xivi\Core\Twig`), so a card
	draws a customer's values through the same field types the record page does
	rather than through a formatting rule of its own.

	**The page is as long as the number of topics in use.** Each card stops at
	its ceiling and a card is only drawn when something is in it, so a fresh
	tenant sees no boxes and a busy one sees a card per topic somebody has
	actually filed something under. There is deliberately no pager: paging over
	cards would cut a topic in half across two pages, and the way past one card's
	ceiling is that card's own link.
#}
<div class="row row-cols-1 row-cols-md-2 row-cols-xl-3 g-3">
	{% for card in cards %}
		<div class="col">
			<div class="card h-100">
				<div class="card-header d-flex align-items-baseline justify-content-between gap-2">
					{#
						The customer's own word for the topic. `label` is null in the
						two cases nothing named them: the entries that answered with
						nothing, which get the field's own name in a sentence, and a
						value left over from an option that has gone, which is shown
						raw because the raw value is the only honest thing left to
						call it.
					#}
					<span class="fw-semibold">
						{%- if card.label is not null -%}
							{{ card.label }}
						{%- elseif card.isUnfiled -%}
							{{ 'index.unfiled'|trans({'%field%': field.label}, 'knowledge') }}
						{%- else -%}
							{{ card.value }}
						{%- endif -%}
					</span>
					{# What this reader may see under it, never what exists (§8.4). #}
					<span class="badge text-bg-secondary">{{ card.total }}</span>
				</div>
				<ul class="list-group list-group-flush">
					{% for record in card.records %}
						<li class="list-group-item d-flex align-items-baseline justify-content-between gap-2">
							{# The title, and nothing else that can be pressed. Edit and
							   delete are on the record page, which is one click away and
							   already carries both; a card of entries is for reading. #}
							<a class="text-reset" href="{{ urls[record.id] }}">
								{{ record_title(module, record.data, record.id) }}
							</a>
							{#
								**The age comes along** (XIV-132, §5.22). The Changed
								column was added because staleness rather than emptiness
								is what a knowledge base fails at, and an entry from 2023
								describing a process that changed in 2024 is the failure
								that costs something. Dropping the column and keeping
								nothing in its place would have quietly given that up, so
								the date sits at the end of each line: the day alone,
								because a card is scanned, with the machine-readable
								moment on the `datetime` attribute.
							#}
							{% if record.updatedAt %}
								<time class="text-body-secondary small text-nowrap" datetime="{{ record.updatedAt|date('c') }}">
									{{- record.updatedAt|date('Y-m-d') -}}
								</time>
							{% endif %}
						</li>
					{% endfor %}
				</ul>
				{#
					**A card that is showing less than it counted says so, and links
					to the rest.** Silently stopping at ten would make a card of the
					first ten look like a topic with ten entries in it, which is the
					thing XIV-35 refuses. The link is this same index narrowed to
					this topic and asked for as rows, so the ceiling is behind a
					page that has a pager and column headers.
				#}
				{% if card.isTruncated %}
					<div class="card-footer small text-body-secondary">
						{{ 'module.card_shown'|trans({'%shown%': card.shown, '%total%': card.total}, 'messages') }}
						<a href="{{ card.listing }}">{{ 'module.card_all'|trans({}, 'messages') }}</a>
					</div>
				{% endif %}
			</div>
		</div>
	{% endfor %}
</div>
